const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Meta-Analysis Service","serviceType":"Meta-analysis, statistical pooling and evidence synthesis support","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/meta-analysis/","description":"Effect sizes recomputed from source, pooling in R, Stata or RevMan, forest and funnel plots, GRADE certainty. From $480, heterogeneity reported.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the main steps in conducting a meta-analysis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Specify the question and the effect measure; write and register an analysis plan that fixes the pooling model, the subgroups and the sensitivity analyses; search the literature systematically; select studies against pre-set criteria; extract the outcome data and appraise each study's risk of bias; compute effect sizes from source rather than copying them; pool them under the planned model; quantify and then explain the heterogeneity; run the pre-specified sensitivity and subgroup analyses; assess small-study effects; and report the whole thing under PRISMA with the code attached.\n\nThe order matters. Everything up to the pooling should be settled before a single pooled estimate exists, because once you have seen one, every subsequent choice is contaminated by knowing what it does to the answer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does a meta-analysis actually combine results from different studies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By weighting. Each study contributes an effect size — a risk ratio, an odds ratio, a mean difference — and a variance that reflects how precisely it was measured. Studies are then weighted by the inverse of that variance, so a large, precise trial counts for more than a small, noisy one, and the weighted average is the pooled estimate.\n\nThat is the whole mechanism. What differs between methods is what goes into the weight: a fixed-effect model uses precision alone, while a random-effects model adds the estimated between-study variance, which pulls the weights closer together and gives small studies more influence. You can see exactly how much more in the forest plot above."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Fixed effect or random effects — which should I use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Decide from the question, in the protocol, and not from the output. A fixed-effect model assumes every study is estimating the same underlying effect and that differences between them are chance; that is defensible when the trials are near-replicates of each other, and rarely otherwise. A random-effects model assumes the true effect varies across settings and estimates that variation, which is usually the more honest assumption in clinical and social research.\n\nThe trap is that the two models answer slightly different questions and often give different intervals, so picking whichever produced the narrower one is a form of p-hacking that a good reviewer will spot. Report the planned model as the primary analysis and the other as a sensitivity analysis. Where they agree, say so; where they disagree, that disagreement is a result."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is I², and how much heterogeneity is too much?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"I² is the proportion of the variation between study results that is more than chance would produce. The familiar thresholds — below 40% low, 30 to 60% moderate, 50 to 90% substantial — are deliberately overlapping in the Cochrane Handbook because they are a prompt, not a rule.\n\nTwo things are worth knowing. First, I² describes inconsistency, not magnitude: a very large I² among trials that all show benefit matters far less than a moderate one among trials pointing in opposite directions. Second, I² rises with the precision of the studies, so a set of large trials can look more heterogeneous than a set of small ones showing the same spread. The right response is never a threshold; it is to explain the heterogeneity through pre-specified subgroups or meta-regression, and to report a prediction interval alongside the confidence interval."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How few studies can I pool?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two, technically, and that is usually a bad idea. With fewer than about five studies the between-study variance cannot be estimated reliably, which makes a conventional random-effects interval far too narrow — the Hartung–Knapp adjustment exists for exactly this and should be used.\n\nBelow ten studies, funnel plots and Egger's test have too little power to say anything, and meta-regression is close to meaningless. None of this means a small meta-analysis is not worth doing; it means the limitations are real, they belong in the discussion, and the analysis should not be dressed up with tests its size cannot support. We will tell you at the scoping stage how many studies your question is likely to yield and what that allows."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle publication bias and small-study effects?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A funnel plot first, then Egger's or Begg's test where there are ten or more studies, and trim-and-fill or a selection model where the asymmetry needs quantifying rather than just describing.\n\nThe important caveat is one most papers skip: funnel asymmetry is not the same thing as publication bias. It can also be produced by a genuine difference between small and large trials — different populations, different intensity of intervention, poorer conduct in smaller studies — and distinguishing the two takes judgement rather than a test. The stronger defence is built earlier: searching trial registries and grey literature for the studies that were run and never published, which is why registry searching is part of the search rather than an optional extra."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I run a subgroup analysis if the overall result was not significant?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You can, and how you report it decides whether anyone believes it. A subgroup analysis is credible when it was pre-specified in the protocol, when there are few enough of them to be plausible, when the direction was predicted in advance, and when the claim is supported by a test for interaction between the subgroups rather than by one subgroup reaching significance while another did not.\n\nThat last point is the one that sinks most papers: two subgroups can easily produce p = 0.03 and p = 0.20 while the difference between them is nowhere near significant, and reporting that as a subgroup effect is a statistical error rather than a matter of emphasis. Subgroup findings discovered after the fact can still be reported — as hypothesis-generating, labelled as such."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if the studies report their outcomes differently?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Some differences are arithmetic and some are fatal. Medians with interquartile ranges can be converted to means and standard deviations where the distribution allows it, with the method named; missing standard deviations can be derived from confidence intervals, standard errors or p-values; different scales measuring the same construct can be combined as a standardised mean difference; and cluster or cross-over designs can be adjusted before pooling.\n\nWhat cannot be repaired is a set of studies measuring genuinely different things — infection at seven days and infection at ninety, or three unrelated quality-of-life instruments — because pooling those produces a precise number that means nothing. Where that is the case we say so before running anything, and the paper becomes a structured narrative synthesis reported under SWiM, which is the harder and more honest answer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is meta-analysis important in evidence-based research?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because individual studies are usually too small to settle anything. A trial powered to detect a large effect will often fail to detect a real but moderate one, and a literature full of such trials looks like disagreement when it is mostly noise. Pooling increases statistical power, narrows the interval and turns that apparent disagreement into a single estimate with a stated uncertainty — which is what a guideline committee, an HTA body such as NICE, CADTH or IQWiG, or a regulator can actually act on — including inside the systematic literature review behind an EU Joint Clinical Assessment dossier, reported to ISPOR good practice. It also makes the disagreement itself measurable: heterogeneity, subgroup differences and small-study effects are all things you can only see once the studies are in the same analysis."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is named as an author, and do I get the analysis code?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You are the author, and yes. Authorship under ICMJE requires substantial contribution to conception or design, or to acquisition, analysis or interpretation; drafting or critically revising; approving the final version; and agreeing to be accountable for it. Pubrica supports all four but takes none of them in your place, and we do not accept authorship on client work.\n\nWhere our contribution should appear in the acknowledgements — and on an analysis we ran, it should — we say so and draft the wording. The commented R scripts, Stata do-files or RevMan project come to you with the manuscript, along with the extraction spreadsheet and the provenance of every derived number, so you can answer a reviewer asking for one more sensitivity analysis without coming back to us. Any AI-assisted screening or drafting is declared in the methods, as ICMJE and COPE now require."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a prediction interval, and why does it matter more than the confidence interval?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The confidence interval tells you how precisely the average effect across these studies has been estimated. The prediction interval tells you what effect a new study in a new setting might plausibly find, and it incorporates the between-study variance rather than averaging it away.\n\nWhen heterogeneity is real the two can point in different directions: a pooled estimate that excludes no effect, with a prediction interval that comfortably includes it. That is not a contradiction, it is the honest answer — the average is real and the next trial could still find nothing. We report both where there are enough studies to estimate one, and we say plainly which of the two the clinical claim rests on."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I meta-analyse observational studies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You can, and the questions it raises are different from those in a meta-analysis of trials. Confounding does not average out across studies; if every included cohort adjusted for the same incomplete set of covariates, pooling them produces a precise estimate of the same bias.\n\nSo the appraisal has to be at the domain level with ROBINS-I V2, the adjustment sets have to be compared rather than assumed equivalent, and the analysis usually pools adjusted rather than crude estimates and says which adjustment each one carried. MOOSE governs the reporting. Where the studies adjusted for materially different things, the defensible output is often a structured synthesis rather than a single number."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a network meta-analysis, and when do I need one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"When several treatments exist but few have been compared head to head, a network meta-analysis uses the trials that share a common comparator to estimate the comparisons nobody has run directly. It answers the question a guideline committee, an HTA body or a payer actually asks — which of these, not whether this one works. Where the network will not connect, the indirect treatment comparison is run as a matching-adjusted indirect comparison or a simulated treatment comparison, with the population adjustment and its limitations stated.\n\nThe price is a stronger assumption: transitivity, that the studies are similar enough in the factors that modify the effect for an indirect comparison to be meaningful. That assumption is assessed, not asserted, and consistency between direct and indirect evidence is tested where the network has closed loops. Reported under PRISMA-NMA, with the network geometry shown so a reader can see where the evidence is thin."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need individual participant data?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually not, and it costs considerably more to get. Aggregate data answers most questions about an overall effect well. Individual participant data earns its cost in three situations: when you need consistent subgroup definitions across studies rather than whatever each paper happened to report, when you want time-to-event analysis and the papers give only hazard ratios, and when the studies used different cut-points on a continuous variable. It also lets you check the data rather than trust the published summary, which is occasionally the whole reason for doing it. The obstacle is rarely analytical; it is obtaining agreements from every trial group, and that timeline should be scoped before the protocol is registered."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do we update a meta-analysis when new trials appear?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Decide the rule in advance, because the alternative is re-running the analysis whenever a result you like appears. A living systematic review sets a search cadence — monthly or quarterly — and an explicit threshold for when the conclusion is revisited.\n\nWhere the concern is repeated testing inflating the false positive rate, trial sequential analysis or a similar sequential method sets the boundary in advance and tells you whether the evidence has actually accumulated to the point of a firm answer or merely passed 0.05 on this update. Either way the update is dated, the search is re-run in full rather than topped up, and the previous version stays available."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Meta-analysis","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/meta-analysis/"}]}];

